# Cell composition and developmental state in the PMP22 antioxidant contrast
Question: q_b6e71fffe204498a
Prior investigation: q_277f20df4b6b47cc

## Answer
Normal Schwann developmental/state variation explains a substantial component of the shared differentiation response, but does not reproduce the large antioxidant RNA increase in the Nae1 knockout contrast. Cell composition and compartment remain important, incompletely identified contributors. The available reference does not justify estimating actual nerve cell fractions or declaring the antioxidant response Schwann-cell-intrinsic.

The defensible update is a separation of two observations: a state-sensitive Pmp22/myelin decline and an additional antioxidant/redox-associated RNA response in this particular Nae1 experiment. These can coexist without an NRF2-to-PMP22 regulatory link. This follow-up narrows the simple state-only explanation; it does not identify NRF2 activity, the responding cell type, direct transcriptional regulation, or causal specificity of Nae1 versus mTOR perturbation.

